WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:05.569
In 17th century-Europe terrible events were
affecting Jewish communities: The Thirty Years

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:09.610
War, the Cossack revolt, and massacres of
the Jewish people.

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:14.590
They were also accused of “blood libel,”
or the ritual murder of Christian children.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:16.900
They needed a redeemer, a symbol of hope.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:23.199
So they turned back to their folklore and
to the Golem.

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:28.640
This mud and earth-formed humanoid creature
turns up in many tales, and not only in Jewish

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.000
but non-Jewish stories.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.380
Golem stories have survived over time, persisting in written and oral folklore

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:41.480
But it was during time of genocide and war that the story of the golem holds the most significance

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.739
In Jewish folklore, there are three reasons
to create a golem: as a way to show one’s

00:00:54.739 --> 00:00:59.570
mastery of the Kabbalah, to have a trustworthy
and dependable servant for labor, or to provide

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.440
protection for the Jewish community.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.800
You’ve likely seen a golem, even if you
didn’t recognize it at the time.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.689
He’s taken lots of different forms in recent
years, inspiring characters in The Simpsons,

00:01:10.689 --> 00:01:15.960
Pokémon, Dungeons and Dragons, Arthur, and
even the X-Files, but golems can be traced

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.909
back to 6th century Jewish culture.

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:18.909
He’s old.

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:20.810
Old as dirt.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:26.689
The Golem story most widely known is the early
20th century version of the Golem of Prague.

00:01:26.689 --> 00:01:31.350
In it, Rabbi Loew asked the Heavens how to
stop the persecution of his community, and

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:35.110
he received an answer— make a Golem.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:39.350
To do this he seeks the help of two other
rabbis, each “born with” the power of

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.159
one of the four elements.

00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:44.390
The rabbis represented air, fire, and water.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:48.130
Clay collected from the river served as the
earth element.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:51.640
The rabbis take the clay and shape the form
of a man.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:57.310
They each walk around the form counterclockwise
seven times, reciting special letters.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:00.740
The body begins to glow and vapors rise from
the form.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:06.240
The golem opens
his eyes—now a living thing.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.270
The Golem of Prague is named Yosef and looks
human but cannot speak.

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:16.840
Rabbi Loew tells the golem he was created
to guard the Jewish people from their enemies.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:22.400
Yosef has supernatural strength and can become
invisible with the help of a special amulet.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.530
He completes many tasks serving as a watchman,
rescuing a kidnapped girl, and even summoning

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.310
a dead woman.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:34.040
The Golem of Prague is only one version of
the golem.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.130
Others have existed for thousands of years.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:41.510
One could be brought to life by a Jewish scholar,
usually a rabbi, and God’s name is required

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.070
in some way to actually bring it to life.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:49.040
You can animate a golem by reciting the proper
letters of God’s name, placing God’s name

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.740
into the golem’s mouth, affixing it to the
forehead, or by placing the Hebrew word for

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.840
“truth” into the clay body.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:01.470
To understand why the golem is created from
dirt, first we have to take a quick look at

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.380
another creation story.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:07.540
One found in the Torah, the foundational text
of Judaism.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:11.990
There it is said that God created Adam, the
first man, by forming him from the dust of

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:15.290
the earth and breathing life into him.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:20.230
Jewish scholars developed the Golem legend
based on the Adam creation story.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:24.800
Historians point to a section of the Talmud,
where a rabbi created an artificial man who

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:31.180
was like any other, except he could not speak
- for only God could give that power, which

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:35.670
explains why all traditional variations of
the golem are mute.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:40.710
In later versions, the creature becomes dangerous
or hard to control because it gains power

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:45.490
the longer it is animated, or it simply takes
direction too literally.

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:50.110
Looking back to the Golem of Prague, perhaps
the singular most important character in these

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:55.810
stories, even more so than the golem himself,
is Rabbi Loew, a real person and noted leader

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:59.320
of the Jewish community in the 16th Century.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:03.450
Interestingly, Loew himself didn’t believe
in miracles, condemned magic, and never wrote

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:04.450
about the golem.

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:10.340
But In 1909, a manuscript supposedly written
by Rabbi Loew’s son-in-law was “discovered”

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:15.000
by Orthodox Rabbi Yudl Rosenberg - though
it’s generally agreed that this document

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.690
never existed.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:21.109
He then published his version of the Golem
of Prague which became one of the most important

00:04:21.109 --> 00:04:22.650
Golem legends.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:28.030
The 1ate 19th Century was the first time the
Golem became not just a means of labor, but

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:30.820
a protector of the Jewish people.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:36.080
Throughout the 19th century, there were multiple
cases of blood libel, an antisemitic accusation

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:41.740
that claims a Jewish person ritualistically
murdered a Christian, usually a child.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.470
Rabbi Rosenberg was all too familiar with
this.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:49.570
There was a blood libel case near Prague in
1899, as well as organized violence against

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:52.830
the Jewish people in Russia at the turn of
the twentieth century.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:57.460
It makes sense that he would portray the golem
in his story as a protector at a time when

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:59.319
his people really needed one.

00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:04.389
In 1917, The Golem: Legends of the Ghetto
of Prague, the next popular text featuring

00:05:04.389 --> 00:05:06.099
Rabbi Loew, was published.

00:05:06.099 --> 00:05:10.680
The Jewish author was part of the community
forced to relocate to the interior of Austria

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.180
after the Russian invasion.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:16.249
Although similar to Rosenberg’s version,
in this golem story, Rabbi Loew is forced

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:22.490
to destroy the golem, here named Joseph, because
he becomes a danger to the entire city.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.900
Nevertheless, the story ends with the golem
waiting in the attic of a synagogue in Prague

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:31.749
to be reawakened the next time persecuted
Jews need protecting.

00:05:31.749 --> 00:05:36.590
The increase in Jewish migration throughout
the twentieth-century helped spread the golem

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:37.590
story.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:40.919
We’ve seen it in movies, ballet, children’s
books, and comics.

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:44.360
But in each new version, it has continued
to change form.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:49.830
The original goal of creating a Golem was
to make something more powerful than yourself.

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:54.699
In most cases this was not done to oppress
or control anyone, but to give the creator

00:05:54.699 --> 00:05:57.879
and their community agency, even freedom.

00:05:57.879 --> 00:06:03.530
What began as a way to demonstrate mastery
of a religious text, morphed into an unintended

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:06.999
monster, and was later reclaimed as a protector.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Golem’s story is as malleable as the clay
from which it was formed

